@meta
id: 05-11
title: Counts going backwards and to ends
chapter: 05
type: lesson
skills: word-counts, word-b, word-e
par: 45
minutes: 6
vscode: clicking twice, in two different places
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
A count works on any of the six motions, not just `w`. `3b` is three
words back. `2e` is two word-ends forward. Nothing new to learn — but
which one you count matters, because the cheap route to a target is
usually not the obvious direction.

Two changes to this file.

Credits come off the total before discounts do. Line three should read:

    const total = order.net + order.tax - order.credit - order.discount;

And the total is returned in minor units, so line five should read:

    return total * 100;

The first target is far from the start of its line and close to the end.
Approach it from the side that costs least.

@keys
| `3b` | three words back |
| `2e` | two word-ends forward |
| `$` | end of the line |
| `0` | first column of the line |
| `i` `a` | insert before, append after |

@hints
For line three, count from the start and then count from the end. One of
those numbers is much smaller.
--
From `$`, `b` visits `discount`, then the dot, then `order`. Three
presses, and the third is where the new term goes.
--
`2j$3b` then `i`, type `order.credit - `, `Esc`. Then `2j0`, `2e` to reach
the end of `total`, `a`, type ` * 100`, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    2j $ 3b  i order.credit -  <Esc>   2j 0 2e  a * 100<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

The first edit is the lesson. Counted from the start of the line, the
insertion point is the eleventh word. Counted from the end it is the
third. Same place, one press versus a number you would have had to check
twice.

So before you count, look at both ends. Targets near the right edge of a
line belong to `$` and `b`; targets near the left belong to `w` and `e`.
This sounds obvious written down and is routinely ignored at speed.

The second edit shows the other half: `0` resets you to a known place, so
a small count is reliable. Counting from wherever the cursor happened to
be left is how people end up pressing a motion, squinting, and pressing
it again.

@start order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async total(order: Order) {
    const total = order.net + order.tax - order.discount;

    return total;
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async total(order: Order) {
    const total = order.net + order.tax - order.credit - order.discount;

    return total * 100;
  }
}
